WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
J W T.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:05.626
A token that any service can verify without a database.

3
00:00:05.626 --> 00:00:08.200
But there's one rule you must never forget.

4
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.133
A JSON Web Token is a signed, self contained token.

5
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:18.567
The payload carries claims: who the user is, when the token expires, and maybe roles.

6
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:21.853
Any service holding the key can verify it locally.

7
00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:23.167
That's stateless auth.

8
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:25.500
Here's the anatomy.

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.533
The header names the algorithm.

10
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:29.467
The payload holds the claims.

11
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.657
And the signature covers both.

12
00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:34.933
Flip one character anywhere, and verification fails.

13
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.467
Three habits keep JWTs safe.

14
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.100
They're tamper evident.

15
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.358
Verify properly, checking the signature and pinning the expected algorithms.

16
00:00:45.358 --> 00:00:49.533
The classic attacks exploit sloppy verification, not the crypto.

17
00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:55.133
And keep access tokens short lived, since you can't easily revoke one before it expires.

18
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:57.367
Let's try one.

19
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:01.567
We encode claims for a user, with an expiry fifteen minutes away.

20
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:05.600
Decoding with the key returns the claims, and we pin the algorithm.

21
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.311
Now we alter the last two characters.

22
00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:11.633
Verification fails with an invalid signature error.

23
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:14.067
Here's how it scales.

24
00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:22.767
Your platform has three FastAPI services: the RAG API, the telemetry API, and the exoplanet classifier.

25
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:27.767
Users log in once and get a fifteen minute token with role and missions claims.

26
00:01:28.067 --> 00:01:30.947
Each service verifies the signature locally.

27
00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:33.500
No auth round trip, no session cluster.

28
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.893
The mistake: putting secrets in the payload because the token is signed.

29
00:01:38.893 --> 00:01:40.000
Anyone can read it.

30
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:46.633
Keep secrets out, expire tokens in minutes, and rotate refresh tokens to bound the damage of a leak.

31
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:48.752
Let's recap.

32
00:01:48.752 --> 00:01:53.267
A JWT is header, payload and signature, all self contained.

33
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.733
Any service can verify it locally.

34
00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:58.400
Signed means tamper evident, not secret.

35
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.598
So keep tokens short lived.

36
00:02:00.598 --> 00:02:02.567
Next up: Linux fundamentals.
